### Legacy FsCap emit-c — PASS (static)

```
pub fn main(fs: &FsCap) { let r = read_file(fs, "/etc/hosts"); … }
```
- emit: `long long fs = oo_cap_grant_fs();` + `oo_read_file(fs, …)`
- runtime: `require_fsread` accepts `g_tok_fs` → seal compatible

### Correct FsReadCap emit-c — product residual FAIL

```
pub fn main(fsread: &FsReadCap) { let r = read_file(fsread, "/etc/hosts"); … }
```
- emit: `oo_read_file(fsread, …)` **without** `long long fsread = oo_cap_grant_fsread();`
- main inject hardcodes legacy set only (`fs/sys/env/net/time/rand/alloc/ffi/thread/gpu`) — no `fsread`/`fswrite` idents
- gcc of emit-c alone: `'fsread' undeclared` (same class as CAP-G1 HttpCap residual)
- **Conclusion:** runtime can; product emit host cannot until CAP-G3 grant matrix wires `FsReadCap`/`FsWriteCap` main params

### `oodac run` honesty note

`run` dispatches to **VM bytecode** (`vm_run_bytecode_caps`), not Backend-C runtime seal. VM host grants only `&FsCap`/`&SysCap` for the interpreter process. Do **not** treat `oodac run` stdout as C-runtime token proof. C-host probe is the CAP-G2 runtime evidence.

